findings.detail:
From phase-d-security-3.md (FINAL), which has no blocking, major or minor findings.

- SEC-D-18: alignedAt treats only ASCII digits and ',' '.' ':' as part of a number, and normalizeForCompare keeps format characters. So when the buyer's own text has one of the forms below, a part of the number still grounds (ev:ev-mv36kohb-016711c9, N2-N6, N10 through the route):
  - '1 200' with a space, NBSP or narrow NBSP;
  - "1'200";
  - the Arabic comma '١،٢٠٠';
  - an invisible character inside the number ('2\u200b500');
  - an unfolded look-alike digit ('２500').
  I confirm the space and U+200B cases in my own spot check. The model chooses only the quote and cannot add the character: a quote holding a character that is not in the text is NOT_QUOTED. This is the lexical class of dec-mv36ch5t-01a1d509, and the buyer confirms every field.
- SEC-D-19: MERIDIEM_TOKEN's optional final '.' makes the token in 'by 3 pm.' end after the full stop, so the quote 'by 3 pm' does not contain it. The correct deadline is unknown and asked again. This is the safe direction, and usability only. d3's test text 'cups by 3 pm please' (extract.test.js L308) does not hit it. I confirm it in my spot check.
- SEC-D-17 note: d3's TR-D-16 real-server tests give the server '<stub bin>:/usr/bin:/bin' (uploads-retention.test.js L821) without the 'no claude in /usr/bin or /bin' assertion that live-runner-refusal.test.js makes. This is safe as written, because the stub is first.

Unchanged. Carry item 6 now also lists:
- SEC-D-13: lexical grounding, dec-mv36ch5t-01a1d509;
- SEC-D-14: capacity and diameter from the photo. My P5 re-run still stores capacityMl 1999 'image' known, as dec-mv35aank-0169ec16 allows.

resolvedFindings.resolution:
Resolved; this is also security's SEC-D-11. d1's validateExtraction requires the quote to have at least one occurrence aligned to whole numbers (schema.js L306, L349-L372). Before a quote that starts with a digit or separator, there may be no digit, and no separator preceded by a digit; the same holds after a quote that ends in one. Clock times are masked before numbers are read (L333-L336). d3 inherits the rule through quoteGrounded, so there is a single source.

The conditions:
- the alignment rule is as I asked;
- the P3b cases are unknown through the route: my P3b re-run by path (ev:ev-mv36npp3-01cc274c) shows cupQuantity 200 from '1,200 cups', lidQuantity 200 from '1200 lids' and capacityMl 50 from '250 ml' unknown, while diameter 90 stays known;
- '250' from '2500 cups' is refused in d1's suite (schema.test.js L227) and in my P7;
- the SEC-7 route test extract.test.js L436 covers 200 from '1,200';
- '1,200 cups'->1200, '250ml'->250, '$120.50'->120.5 and '٢٥٠ مل'->250 stay known in P7 (ev:ev-mv36n2by-01c422d2).

Mutants M15, M16, M17 and M18 are killed (ev:ev-mv36sh0c-019c0ced). My condition named ',' '.' '٫' '٬'. The rarer buyer-written grouping forms that security found are info (TR-D-19 / SEC-D-18).

Resolved; this is also security's SEC-D-16. d2 was reset. A call ended by close() rejects PROVIDER_UNAVAILABLE with the per-call cap as its usage, in the full usage shape (cli.js L246, L287). gate.run then settles at the cap.

The real gate through the route:
- my P10a (ev:ev-mv36n2by-01c422d2) and my P3c re-run (ev:ev-mv36npp3-01cc274c) show the row reserved 50000 while the stub runs, and settled 50000/50000 after close(); the response is 200, degraded, PROVIDER_UNAVAILABLE;
- on a real server, P4 SIGTERM and P11 SIGHUP leave the row settled at the cap, and so do security's S1-S9 (ev:ev-mv36rmvf-01b7b830);
- P4 SIGKILL leaves the row reserved, which is the FU-28 residual the startup sweep settles.

The suite's regression guard is cli.test.js L437 (the real gate and SQLite). The HTTP-level row test that carry item 8 says nobody wrote is replaced, as evidence for this phase, by my P3c, P10a and P11 runs and by security's S-runs. Mutants M22, M19b and M19c are killed. M19 and M21 survive (TR-D-18, info).

FU-32: I accept the extension, as dec-mv36cgqs-01f68eb5 records, and security accepts it too. Once a child has started, the real spend is unknown and bounded by the cap, so charging the cap on the adapter's own timeout, an abort or a flood keeps the ledger conservative, and it matches the service watchdog. The evidence:
- P10b and security's F1 show the timeout settled at the cap; F2 shows the same for the flood;
- F3 and F4 show a reported cost still wins;
- P10c shows a call on a closed provider spawns nothing and settles at 0.

Correction to FU-32's stated side effect, which security found and I confirm by reading the code: no HTTP path aborts the provider call. The only controller.abort() is the watchdog's (extraction.js L298); the 'aborted' listeners belong to the upload reader. A buyer who disconnects is therefore not charged the cap for leaving. delivery-lead states the rule in the verification report as: started and killed means the cap; not started means 0; reported means reported.

